    "result_text": "The change is complete and the suite is green.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe requirement: `POST /api/owners` responses must carry `bulkSignupWarning: true` once more than 80 owners already exist for today's registration date, otherwise `false`. The acceptance test also reads the field back via `GET /api/owners/{id}` and expects `false` for a fresh create.\n\nTwo files touched:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `bulkSignupWarning` boolean to the `Owner` schema with `default: false`. The generated `OwnerDto` now has the field initialized to `false`, so every mapped response (including the `GET` path, whose mapper leaves it at its default) serializes `false` unless explicitly set.\n\n2. **`RespondWithOwnerCreated.java`** \u2014 the create responder now counts owners whose `registrationDate` equals today's rolled business day (the same accumulation the daily-limit rule uses) and sets `bulkSignupWarning` to `count > 80`.\n\n## Keeping structural impact low\n\n- Only **2 files** touched \u2014 the largest lever in the cost formula is the file multiplier, so I avoided the dedicated-step-plus-YAML-wiring approach (4 files) and did not touch `OwnerMapper` (its large `WMC_other` would dominate the cost).\n- The one changed function, `service`, stays branch-free (CC unchanged: `count > 80` is an expression, the lambda has no decision points), and it's the sole method in its class so its `WMC_other` is minimal.\n- The `GET`-path `false` default comes for free from the schema's `default: false`, so no second endpoint or mapper edit was needed.\n\n`AcceptanceTest` (the only test class) and the full `mvn test` build both pass.",
